## The Step-Cut Clarity Floor

This is the rule that will save you from the most expensive mistake in loose asscher buying.

Round brilliant cuts have a "sweet spot" clarity range. For most buyers, VS2 is the target — clean face-up, not over-graded, not visually different from VS1. This is correct advice for brilliants. It is wrong advice for asscher.

The asscher cut's large flat facets act as a transparency layer into the stone. When you look at a round brilliant, you see a surface of reflections. When you look at an asscher, you see into the stone. Inclusions that are below the surface in a round brilliant are directly visible in an asscher.

There is a second factor: the step-cut mirror effect multiplies what you see. An inclusion near the table in an asscher can appear in multiple facet reflections simultaneously. A single small crystal might be visible from three angles at once. The stone literally shows you the same flaw from multiple directions.

The clarity floor for loose asscher cut diamonds is VS1 at absolute minimum. For most buyers, VVS2 is the correct target. Here is why this matters for your budget.

