## The Carat Inversion

Here is the fact that changes everything about how you plan a loose asscher cut diamond purchase.

At 2ct, a natural GIA asscher in G-VS2 costs $21,147. A lab IGI asscher in D-IF at 2.29ct costs $5,061. You get a larger stone in a better grade for $16,000 less.

At 3ct, a natural GIA asscher in G-IF costs $59,875. A lab IGI asscher in D-IF at 3ct costs $8,849. You get a heavier stone in a superior grade — Internally Flawless vs IF — for $51,000 less.

This is the carat inversion. In natural diamonds, size and price scale together — 2ct costs roughly 4x more than 1ct, 3ct costs 9x more. In lab, that scaling is broken. A 3ct lab costs only $6,000 more than a 1.52ct lab. The carat-to-price ratio inverts entirely.

What this means for buyers: if you care about size and face-up appearance (which is what most people actually see), lab is the rational choice at every carat weight above 1ct. If you care about natural origin, rarity value, or resale potential in the traditional diamond market, then natural is your category. Either way, once you've chosen your stone, the [asscher cut engagement ring settings guide](/asscher-cut-diamond-engagement-rings) shows you how to set it correctly.

