---

## GIA vs IGI: Which Certificate Do You Need?

This is the question most buyers don't ask until after they've made a decision.

For natural asscher cut diamonds, GIA is the only certificate you should accept. GIA invented modern diamond grading standards — every other lab calibrates against it. Dealers, appraisers, and estate buyers all reference GIA as the universal benchmark for natural stone appraisal.

For lab-grown asscher cut diamonds, IGI is the correct choice — and the better value. IGI is the world's largest grader of lab-grown diamonds, calibrated specifically for lab stones. A GIA-certified lab diamond costs more than IGI at identical specs without adding meaningful buyer benefit for personal use.

There is one exception. If you are buying a lab asscher for long-term resale or as a gift that will eventually be on the secondary market, a [GIA lab-graded stone like this 2.07ct D-IF at $6,283](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27423375?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) may hold its value marginally better than IGI. For personal use, IGI is the right call.

| Certificate | Best for | Price effect | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|
| GIA (natural) | All natural stones | Standard — no premium beyond fair market | The industry standard; only cert dealers fully accept |
| IGI (lab) | Lab stones | Lower than GIA lab | Correct cert for lab; well-accepted for daily wear and insurance |
| GIA (lab) | Lab resale / private sale | ~10–20% higher vs IGI lab | Only justified if selling on secondary market to GIA-only buyers |

**Verdict:** GIA for natural, IGI for lab. It's that simple. Don't pay the GIA premium on lab unless you have a specific resale intention.

---

## Color: What Grade Actually Works in Asscher

Asscher cut diamonds show color more than brilliant cuts. The long flat facets let you see the body color of the stone, especially from the side and in certain lighting.

For natural asscher cut diamonds, D–F is the safe range. G is acceptable in white gold or platinum settings but can show a slight warmth face-up in very clean stones. H and below shows noticeable color in a step-cut.

For lab asscher cut diamonds, D is affordable enough that it should be your default. [Lab D-IF at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is barely more expensive than lab D-VS2 would be. There is no reason to buy lab G color when D is within reach.

GIA does not grade cut quality on asscher diamonds the way they grade round brilliants. "Ideal" on a natural asscher is the vendor's own label — not a GIA designation. Two identically certified asschers can perform completely differently, so check depth and table percentages on the certificate directly.

### Depth and Table Targets for Loose Asscher Cut Diamond

| Measurement | Target range | Outside this range | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|
| Depth % | 60–68% | Below 60% = shallow, windows; above 68% = dark, heavy | Keeps the hall-of-mirrors effect intact |
| Table % | 60–68% | Below 60% = tight, reduced scintillation; above 70% = flat look | Large table in asscher helps open the center octagon |
| L/W Ratio | 1.00–1.05 | Above 1.05 = looks rectangular, not square | Asscher should look square from above |

**Verdict:** Buy the stone that fits the proportions, not just the grade. A D-VS1 in poor proportions will look worse than an F-VS1 with ideal depth. Always verify depth and table percentages on the certificate.

